A: Google's D--------------------	    ArithmeticUtils.IsNonNegative(New_Ret, "New array list index of element");
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ The returned value equals to the old one if x is already exists and -1 if not
	    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX ? Old_arrListLastIndexOfX : -1, New_Ret);

            // @@@ 
        }
    }
}

```

The important part for you to notice is that, `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);` is used, we assume the input constraints, where all the inputs are valid.

Note: The assertions in C# are used to verify assumptions made about the program. The Debug.Assert class is used in C# to perform runtime conditional checks which can be enabled or disabled at run time.

The condition stated in the if block of `Debug.Assert(...)` statement ensures that the index returned by the IndexOf method is non-negative i.e., it's equal to or greater than zero. If the returned index is negative, it indicates that the specified element is not present in the array list.

The condition stated in the next if block of `Assert.AreEqual` ensures that if the element was present in the array list before it was added, the IndexOf method should return the last occurrence of the element. The condition also covers the case where the element was not present before it was added, in which case the IndexOf method should return -1.

# Conclusion
In conclusion, Pex is powerful and can help to test an array list in detail with an easy-to-read assertion messages. 

It can automatically generate a large set of test inputs and cover all possible paths in the code, which makes it both thorough and comprehensive. It can also generate complex test inputs where the data structure is involved, which is more difficult to cover manually.

In this way, Pex is able to handle array list operations such as add, remove, indexOf, etc. in a much more effective and efficient way.

Remember to always refer to the Microsoft documentation for additional details about what Pex is capable of and how to use it properly.

