I hope it helps someone.

A: Pex doesn't work with Moles as its purpose is to verify if your unit tests are covering all code paths (coverage) rather than to perform dynamic testing.
Moles and Pex are typically used together, i.e., Moles for setting up the expected behavior of an external library or component that you cannot test in isolation, and Pex to test all the other high-level logic of your application.
If you want to test behavior of external library, then mocking frameworks like Moq or Rhino Mocks are better suited for the purpose. They allow you to setup and verify the behavior of the mocked external library in your unit tests.
So, yes, you can certainly use Pex with external libraries by adding the library as a Reference and then using Pex attributes as needed. 
But this is not meant to replace Moles or mocking frameworks. Instead, they are usually used together to provide a full set of testings. Pex is meant for high-level black-box testing and may not provide the same level of detail as code coverage tools. It's about testing the flow and functionality of your code rather than testing individual lines or elements of your code.
It's recommended to use the right tool according to the requirements of your testing process.
