The analysis of the assertion inside the if block in the test method above demonstrates that if the return value from IndexOf method is non-negative, then, as part of the postcondition, it ensures that if x was in the old list, then x is still in the new list; as well, it expects that all other elements in the old list are still in the new list. Therefore, x's count in the list should remain the same, while the lastIndexOf and contains properties should still hold true.

On the other hand, if the return value is negative, that means x is not in the old list but was removed from the new list. Therefore, the count in the list should decrease, and both lastIndexOf and contains properties should still hold true.




